<h2>The doctrine underneath</h2>
<p>Three features of the 1996 Act&rsquo;s post-2015 architecture make this choreography the norm rather than the exception:</p>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Provision</th><th>Role</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 9(1)</td><td>Court-granted interim measures before and during arbitration, and after the award before enforcement</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 9(3)</td><td>Once the tribunal is constituted, the court &ldquo;shall not entertain&rdquo; a Section 9 application unless the Section 17 remedy is inefficacious</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 17</td><td>The tribunal&rsquo;s interim powers, co-extensive with the court&rsquo;s, with orders deemed enforceable as court orders</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The inefficacy exception is narrow — typically engaged where the tribunal cannot act in time, a vacancy exists, or relief is needed against third parties beyond the tribunal&rsquo;s reach. None of that applied once the sole arbitrator stood ready; the Court accordingly expressed no view on merits and left even the fate of its own status quo order to the tribunal.</p>

<h2>Practice pointers for the handover moment</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p><strong>Ask for conversion, not dismissal.</strong> A Section 9 petition disposed of &ldquo;as a Section 17 application to be considered afresh&rdquo; carries the record and the urgency to the tribunal; a bare dismissal restarts everything.</p>
  <p><strong>Secure continuity expressly.</strong> The interim order should be directed to continue &ldquo;until the tribunal considers the matter&rdquo; — the formula used here — so no unprotected gap opens.</p>
  <p><strong>Unfreeze the tribunal formally.</strong> Arbitrators understandably defer when court proceedings about their own mandate are pending; a short application for directions, as filed here, is the right tool rather than informal correspondence.</p>
  <p><strong>Timetable the Section 17 hearing.</strong> The two-week direction to the arbitrator prevents the converted application from languishing — ask for it.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This note summarises an order of the Delhi High Court for general information. It is not legal advice; interim-measure strategy depends on each arbitration&rsquo;s stage and stakes.</div>
